WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:05.000
So it's going to look like this.

00:05.000 --> 00:08.840
We have our database and this time we don't have only one CTE.

00:09.160 --> 00:12.160
We have multiple cities in our query.

00:12.160 --> 00:15.160
And each CTE is going directly to the database.

00:15.160 --> 00:20.160
And it will query the database in order to prepare the intermediate results.

00:20.160 --> 00:26.840
So in this example for CTE is going to the database and preparing for different intermediate results.

00:26.840 --> 00:29.640
And of course SQL can execute it from the top to the bottom.

00:29.640 --> 00:32.120
So first the city one then 234.

00:32.160 --> 00:34.080
But they have nothing to do with each others.

00:34.080 --> 00:38.880
So now once we have all the four intermediate results, the main query is going to go and retrieve all

00:38.880 --> 00:44.280
those informations and do some magic in order to prepare the final result for the end user.

00:44.280 --> 00:49.760
So now by looking to this sketch, you can understand all those cities are independent from each others.

00:49.760 --> 00:51.760
So there is no nesting or something.

00:52.040 --> 00:58.640
Each CTE is self-contained and it could be executed on its own without depending on any other results

00:58.640 --> 01:01.660
from any other CTE or any other query.

01:01.660 --> 01:04.940
So it goes directly to the database and get the data.

01:04.940 --> 01:07.860
So that's why all of them are standalone cities.

01:07.900 --> 01:12.300
And since we have multiple cities then it is standalone multiple cities.

01:12.340 --> 01:12.780
That's it.

01:12.820 --> 01:13.380
It's simple.

01:13.380 --> 01:16.340
So now let's check the syntax of the multiple standalone cities.

01:16.340 --> 01:18.660
So we're going to start writing our first city.

01:18.860 --> 01:20.740
So it starts with the with clause.

01:20.740 --> 01:22.460
And then we have the city name.

01:22.460 --> 01:24.300
And then the logic of our city.

01:24.340 --> 01:25.340
So nothing new.

01:25.380 --> 01:27.300
This is how we define the city.

01:27.340 --> 01:32.780
And then in order to use it we're going to have our main query where we select from our new city, and

01:32.780 --> 01:35.340
we make sure we are using the name of our city.

01:35.380 --> 01:36.500
So nothing new.

01:36.540 --> 01:41.780
Now, in order to add another city to our query, what we're going to do, we're going to go after the

01:41.780 --> 01:43.220
definition of the city.

01:43.500 --> 01:47.180
And below it we're going to go and start defining the city.

01:47.220 --> 01:47.500
Two.

01:47.540 --> 01:51.620
But this time, as you can see, we are not using the With clause.

01:51.660 --> 01:53.020
We are using a comma.

01:53.020 --> 01:57.260
So that means only the first city is going to be using the with clause.

01:57.260 --> 02:03.720
In order to tell SQL we are talking about CTE, all the other cities, you can separate it using the

02:03.720 --> 02:04.120
comma.

02:04.120 --> 02:07.120
So the syntax is going to be comma instead of width.

02:07.400 --> 02:09.160
Then the name of the city.

02:09.160 --> 02:11.880
And then we're going to say as the following definition.

02:11.880 --> 02:15.240
So we're going to write here the query of the second CTE.

02:15.400 --> 02:20.080
So now of course if you want to go and add more cities you go and use the comma below it.

02:20.080 --> 02:22.320
And as well you define the third city.

02:22.360 --> 02:26.880
So you can have as much cities as you want and always separated with comma.

02:27.000 --> 02:29.400
But only the first city starts with the width.

02:29.440 --> 02:34.800
And of course in the main query we can go and use the results from the CTE two where we are for example

02:34.840 --> 02:37.800
here joining the data between the city one and city two.

02:37.840 --> 02:43.000
So as you can see in the main query here, we are like collecting the data from these different cities

02:43.040 --> 02:46.080
in order to do the final step in the main query.

02:46.120 --> 02:47.240
It starts with the width.

02:47.240 --> 02:50.000
So SQL understands okay now we are talking about CTE.

02:50.200 --> 02:56.480
And once SQL sees after the parenthesis a comma SQL can understand okay now we are talking about another

02:56.480 --> 02:57.000
city.

02:57.040 --> 03:02.140
And now if you don't go and use a comma after the parenthesis SQL can understand okay, we don't have

03:02.140 --> 03:03.020
any more seats.

03:03.300 --> 03:05.940
The next query is about the main query.

03:05.940 --> 03:09.060
So this is how you create multiple standalone seats.

03:09.100 --> 03:09.460
All right.

03:09.460 --> 03:13.340
So now back to our task where we are creating like a report step by step.

03:13.380 --> 03:19.780
So now we have in the task a second step where it says find the last order date for each customer.

03:19.780 --> 03:23.100
So now we have to go and add one more information about our customer.

03:23.100 --> 03:25.820
So when the last time the customer did order.

03:25.820 --> 03:27.100
So how are we going to do it.

03:27.100 --> 03:29.300
Now we have to add this to our query.

03:29.300 --> 03:33.300
And I would like to use as well the CTE in order to have this logic.

03:33.300 --> 03:38.740
So as we learned from the first task, this is the first step in order to find the total sales for each

03:38.740 --> 03:39.260
customer.

03:39.260 --> 03:40.940
And here we have the main query.

03:40.980 --> 03:44.740
Now I would like to put now in between another CTE.

03:44.780 --> 03:48.220
And as we learned from the syntax, we have to go and add a comma.

03:48.220 --> 03:51.500
We cannot go and use the width again and we have to give it a name.

03:51.500 --> 03:55.340
So let's call it CTE and last order.

03:55.660 --> 04:00.380
So like this and we have to define it so as and then double parenthesis.

04:00.660 --> 04:03.230
And now in between we have to go and add our logic.

04:03.230 --> 04:05.150
So now we have to focus only in this logic.

04:05.150 --> 04:09.070
So forget about the other cities and the main query.

04:09.070 --> 04:12.230
So we have to find the last order date for each customer.

04:12.470 --> 04:15.990
So we're going to go and query again the table orders.

04:16.350 --> 04:17.070
So what do we need.

04:17.110 --> 04:18.630
We need the customer ID.

04:18.990 --> 04:25.590
We need the order date from our table sales orders.

04:25.830 --> 04:27.070
So that's it for now.

04:27.110 --> 04:30.150
Let's just select it and execute it.

04:30.150 --> 04:34.150
And now with that you can see all the customers and as well all the orders.

04:34.150 --> 04:37.510
But we would like to have the highest order for each customer.

04:37.510 --> 04:41.830
And we can go and use our aggregate function the max function.

04:41.830 --> 04:44.070
So what we're going to do, it's like here at the top.

04:44.590 --> 04:49.990
So we have to go and use the function max and group up by that customer ID.

04:50.270 --> 04:56.830
So group up the customer ID and let me just shift it like this.

04:56.870 --> 05:00.230
And let's give it the name Last Order.

05:00.230 --> 05:01.110
So like this.

05:01.150 --> 05:04.730
And as you can see I'm just selecting now only my query.

05:04.930 --> 05:10.890
I'm not selecting everything and I keep executing in order just to check the results before we integrate

05:10.890 --> 05:12.490
it in the main query.

05:12.530 --> 05:15.370
So now as you can see we have for each customer one row.

05:15.370 --> 05:18.130
And we have as well the highest order for each customer.

05:18.130 --> 05:20.810
So with that we have solved this subtask.

05:20.810 --> 05:23.090
So as you can see it's really easy to extend.

05:23.130 --> 05:28.730
I'm just making like another box and I'm adding inside it the business logic that I want.

05:28.770 --> 05:32.290
And this can solve one problem from the whole task.

05:32.290 --> 05:34.810
So you feel now exactly the power of the CTE.

05:34.970 --> 05:38.490
We are making complex logic, but still it's easy to add.

05:38.530 --> 05:40.050
Now imagine you are not doing this.

05:40.050 --> 05:42.450
You are always extending one big query.

05:42.490 --> 05:44.250
It's gonna be really hard to extend.

05:44.250 --> 05:51.330
And that's why a lot of SQL developers really love using CTE, and they like use it in each query or

05:51.370 --> 05:53.170
in each task that they have.

05:53.170 --> 05:57.690
So we have solved this task and we have to go now integrate it in the main query.

05:57.690 --> 05:59.170
It's going to be very simple.

05:59.170 --> 06:03.870
So we're going to go over here and we will go and just add another join.

06:03.870 --> 06:05.710
So we're going to join it with the CTE.

06:05.990 --> 06:11.590
And as you can see SQL now is offering it as a table, even though it is not a physical table that exists

06:11.590 --> 06:12.670
in our database.

06:12.950 --> 06:14.910
It only lives inside our data.

06:14.910 --> 06:17.110
But still SQL treats it as a table.

06:17.110 --> 06:18.630
And this is exactly what we are doing.

06:18.630 --> 06:20.870
We treat those informations as table.

06:20.870 --> 06:25.150
So CTE the last order and I will call it CLO.

06:25.270 --> 06:29.270
And then of course we have to go and do the same condition like here.

06:29.590 --> 06:36.590
So the CLO customer ID should be equal to the customer ID from the first table, the customers.

06:36.590 --> 06:40.510
And of course we have to go and add this new information to the main query.

06:40.510 --> 06:43.990
So clo the last order.

06:43.990 --> 06:44.830
So now what we're going to do.

06:44.830 --> 06:46.950
We're going to go and execute the whole thing.

06:46.950 --> 06:50.030
So we have now two seats and as well our main query.

06:50.030 --> 06:51.710
So let's go and execute it.

06:51.750 --> 06:53.150
Now again let's check the data.

06:53.190 --> 06:56.670
The first three columns comes from the physical table customers.

06:56.710 --> 07:01.630
The fourth one the total sales comes from our first CTE over here.

07:02.070 --> 07:03.370
So from here.

07:03.370 --> 07:08.490
And the last order comes from our new city that we just defined the city number two.

07:08.530 --> 07:12.330
So as you can see, guys, everything feels like organized and structured.

07:12.330 --> 07:13.690
And we have like flow.

07:13.730 --> 07:16.890
And of course those cities are standalone cities.

07:17.050 --> 07:21.210
So we can go always and select the city and execute it separately.

07:21.210 --> 07:24.170
It doesn't need anything else from outside this query.

07:24.210 --> 07:27.010
It just needs the tables inside your database.

07:27.010 --> 07:28.690
So guys again here pay attention.

07:28.690 --> 07:34.090
If you want to add more cities use the comma you cannot go and use for example here another width.

07:34.090 --> 07:35.970
So if I execute it I will get an error.

07:36.170 --> 07:38.050
So you have to separate it with comma.

07:38.050 --> 07:45.490
And another mistake that I do frequently is that I forget and go add here like to the last CTE, a comma.

07:45.490 --> 07:48.010
And this happens to me if I'm using a lot of cities.

07:48.210 --> 07:54.970
So if I go and do it like this I will get as well an error because the main query doesn't need a comma.

07:54.970 --> 07:58.170
So the last city should not have a comma after the parenthesis.

07:58.170 --> 08:00.450
So I just removed it and execute.

08:00.450 --> 08:03.930
So guys with that we have now multiple cities inside our query.
